      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
      ":question/problem": "Write the following first-order linear ODE in standard form.\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+4{y}^{2}(\\cos{(2 x )})^{2}=(5-(2)y\\cos{(2 x )})^{2}\\,.\n$$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "A first-order linear ODE is in standard form if it is written as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x).\n$$\n\nConverting the given equation to standard form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+4{y}^{2}(\\cos{(2 x )})^{2}&=(5-(2)y\\cos{(2 x )})^{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+4{y}^{2}(\\cos{(2 x )})^{2}&=25+4{y}^{2}(\\cos{(2 x )})^{2}-20 y \\cos{(2 x )} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}&=25-20 y \\cos{(2 x )} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\underbrace{(20 \\cos{(2 x )})}_{P(x)}y&=\\underbrace{25}_{Q(x)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the equation is written in standard form with $P(x)=20 \\cos{(2 x )}$ and $Q(x)=25.$",
